# 1. EXPAND COMMAND

## What is `expand`?

The `expand` command is used to convert **TAB characters into spaces**.

By default, a tab is converted into spaces according to tab-stop positions.

### Basic Syntax

```bash
expand filename
```

---

## Example

Create a file containing tabs:

```bash
printf 'Name\tAge\tCity\nJohn\t25\tBangalore\n' > data.txt
```

Now use:

```bash
expand data.txt
```

The TAB characters are converted into spaces.

---

## Save the result into another file

```bash
expand data.txt > expanded.txt
```

Now `expanded.txt` contains spaces instead of TAB characters.

---

## Real-world example

Suppose you receive a text file from another system that uses TAB formatting.

Before:

```text
Name    Age    City
John    25     Bangalore
```

You can convert the tabs into spaces:

```bash
expand data.txt
```

This is useful when preparing text files for systems that don't handle tabs consistently.

---

## Practice Exercise

Create a file:

```bash
printf 'Name\tAge\tCountry\nAlice\t22\tIndia\nBob\t25\tUSA\n' > students.txt
```

Run:

```bash
expand students.txt
```

Then save the output:

```bash
expand students.txt > students_spaces.txt
```

