# 11. COMBINING COMMANDS WITH PIPE

One of the most powerful concepts in Linux is combining commands using:

```bash
|
```

This is called a **pipe**.

The output of one command becomes the input of another command.

---

## Example

```bash
cat fruits.txt | sort
```

Flow:

```text
cat
 ↓
sort
 ↓
sorted output
```

---

# Sort + Uniq

```bash
sort fruits.txt | uniq
```

This sorts the file and then removes consecutive duplicate lines.

---

# Sort + Uniq + Count

```bash
sort fruits.txt | uniq -c
```

This is extremely useful for frequency analysis.

---

# Grep + WC

```bash
grep "ERROR" server.log | wc -l
```

Meaning:

```text
grep
 ↓
Find ERROR
 ↓
wc -l
 ↓
Count matching lines
```

---

# Grep + Sort + Uniq

```bash
grep "Failed" auth.log | sort | uniq -c
```

This can help count repeated failed-login messages.

